course: dictionary
lesson: 3514
title: Fatten and fattening up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Fatten means to make something fatter, or to become fatter. Read these clues: «In autumn brown bears gather at rivers to catch salmon swimming upstream, fattening up for winter.» Bears catch lots of salmon in autumn, so they are getting fatter before the cold months come.
explain: The words «fatten up» mean eating well so your body gets fuller and heavier. «Once a year penguins moult all their feathers in a few weeks; they cannot swim or eat then, so they fatten up beforehand and look scruffy.» The penguins get ready early because they cannot eat during the moult.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The hazel dormouse can hibernate for six months or more, after fattening up on hazelnuts and berries in autumn.» Put «eating lots and getting fatter» in place of «fattening up», and the sentence still makes sense. That tells you you have the right meaning.
fact: Fatten means to make an animal or person fatter, or to become fatter.
fact: «Fatten up» means eat plenty so you gain weight, often before a hard time such as winter.
fact: Farmers fatten pigs, cattle and turkeys before they are sold for meat.
fact: «Fattening» describes food that makes you gain weight, as in «rich, fattening food».
fact: Informal: «fatten your wallet» means to make the money you have bigger.
exercise: «In autumn brown bears gather at rivers to catch salmon, fattening up for winter.» What does fattening up mean here? | *Eating well so they grow fatter before winter | Swimming very fast up the river | Sleeping through the whole summer | Catching fish for their friends
exercise: Penguins cannot swim or eat while they moult, so they fatten up beforehand. Why? | To swim faster than other birds | To look smart in a photo | So their feathers grow longer in the sun | *So they have stored body fat before they cannot eat
exercise: Mum says a rich cake is fattening. What does fattening mean here? | Good for your teeth | Made of fruit only | Very cold to eat | *Likely to make you gain weight
test: «The hazel dormouse can hibernate for six months or more, after fattening up on hazelnuts and berries in autumn.» Where does the dormouse fatten up? | *On hazelnuts and berries in autumn | Under a stone in the spring | On the moon in the winter | In the river
test: Which sentence uses fatten in the informal way? | The dormice fatten up in autumn | *Saving every coin will fatten my wallet | Bears fatten up before winter | The farmer will fatten the cattle for market
exam: Why do penguins fatten up before they moult? | They want their feathers to look scruffy for the photo | They are fattening up for a hibernation that lasts six months | *They cannot swim or eat while they moult, so they need stored fat first
